    "title": "SNAP benefits expected to return for 40+ million Americans - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Ryan Arbogast ,\n1 hours ago MANATEE COUNTY, Fla. (WFLA) — Millions of hungry families across the United States will once again receive their Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) benefits following the federal government’s reopening.\nApproximately 41 million families take part in SNAP, and have gone without assistance since Nov. 1, according to the United States Department of Agriculture.\nPresident Trump signs government funding bill, ending shutdown after a record 43-day disruption “What’s happened with rents, what’s happened with gas and the just the cost of food. They’re one episode away of a complete disaster. Do I fix the tire on my car, or do I go buy food? So they rely on our pantries and food distributions. It’s been tough,” said Maribeth Phillips, the CEO of Meals on Wheels Plus of Manatee.\nFood banks across the Tampa Bay area tell 8 On Your Side that amid the shutdown, nonprofits in some locations saw as much as a 50% increase in need. Some organizations conducted weekly giveaways to combat a growing population of families, many of whom had not previously gone to the nonprofit for help.\n“This year has been terrible. I’ve had to go to different pantries all over,” said Carmen Velasquez, who is on SNAP benefits.\nThe United States Department of Agriculture says it hopes federal funding can return to the SNAP program within 24 hours of the government’s reopening.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WFLA.",
